-- title:
--   Node regularity and the slope law from layered crossing presentations
-- statement:
--   Let $L$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero, $A \subseteq L$ a valuation subring with residue field $\kappa$, and $F$ a field extension of $L$ which is essentially of finite type and satisfies `IsCurveOver L F` (principal divisors exist, every place has residue field finite over $L$, and $\Omega_{F/L}$ is free of rank one over $F$). Fix two regular prolongations $R_1, R_2$ of $A$ to $F$, i.e. valuation subrings $R_i.\mathrm{integers} \subseteq F$ cutting out $A$ on $L$, with surjective residue maps onto fields $\bar F_1, \bar F_2$ over $\kappa$ whose kernels are the maximal ideals, compatible with the residue map of $A$, and such that every nonzero $f \in F$ has an $L$-multiple in $R_i.\mathrm{integers}$ with nonzero residue; fix rational places $x_1, x_2$ of $\bar F_1, \bar F_2$ over $\kappa$ (the residue field of the place is generated by $\kappa$). Let $S$ be a set of places of $F/L$, all rational, and $\mathcal N \subseteq F$ a subring consisting exactly of the $f$ lying in $R_1.\mathrm{integers}$, in $R_2.\mathrm{integers}$ and in every valuation subring $P$ with $P \in S$, and assume each $f \in \mathcal N$ has $P$-value in $A$ for all $P \in S$. Assume $A$ has rank one in the form: for $a$ in the maximal ideal of $A$ and $b \neq 0$ in $A$, some $a^n$ is divisible by $b$. Layers of constants: for $l$ in an index type $\Lambda$, subrings $C'_l \subseteq A$ of $L$ which are discrete valuation domains, with elements $\varpi'_l \in C'_l$ such that an element of $C'_l$ has zero residue in $A$ exactly when it is a $C'_l$-multiple of $\varpi'_l$; a base layer $l_0$ with $C'_{l_0} \le C'_l$ for all $l$, $\varpi'_{l_0} \neq 0$, and every element of $A$ algebraic over $C'_{l_0}$. Node data: $x, y, u \in F$ and $E_0 \in \mathbb N$ with $xy = \varpi_{l_0}'^{\,E_0} u$ in $F$. For each $l$ a Noetherian local subring $\mathcal N_{0,l} \le \mathcal N$ such that: $S$ consists precisely of the places $P$ containing $\mathcal N_{0,l}$ and sending every non-unit of $\mathcal N_{0,l}$ into the maximal ideal of $A$ (for each $l$); every $f \in F$ is a quotient $a/b$ with $a, b \in \mathcal N_{0,l}$ for some $l$; $\mathcal N_{0,l_0} \le \mathcal N_{0,l}$; every $f \in F$ satisfies $fb = \sum_i c_i a_i$ with $b \neq 0$, $a_i, b \in \mathcal N_{0,l_0}$ and $c_i \in L$; $C'_l$ maps into $\mathcal N_{0,l}$; every element of $\mathcal N_{0,l}$ differs from an element of $C'_l$ by a non-unit; $\mathcal N_{0,l}$ is linearly disjoint from $L$ over $C'_l$ in the sense that $\sum_i c_i a_i = 0$ with $c_i$ being $C'_l$-linearly independent forces all $a_i = 0$; and $x, y, u \in \mathcal N_{0,l}$ with $u$ a unit there. Crossing presentations: for each $l$ a complete discrete valuation domain $W_l$ with irreducible $\pi_l$, a ring map $\sigma_l : W_l \to \widehat{\mathcal N_{0,l}}$ (adic completion at the maximal ideal) with $\sigma_l(\pi_l)$ the image of $\varpi'_l$, an exponent $E_l \ge 1$, and a ring isomorphism $\iota_l$ of $\widehat{\mathcal N_{0,l}}$ with the crossing model $\mathrm{MvPowerSeries}(\mathrm{Fin}\,2, W_l)/(X_0X_1 - \pi_l^{E_l})$ carrying $\sigma_l(o)$ to `const` $o$, with every element of $C'_l$ in the image of $\sigma_l$, and with the two branch normalisations: if $f \in \mathcal N_{0,l}$ has nonzero $R_1$-residue of $x_1$-order $n$ then $\iota_l(f) - \gamma\, V^n$ lies in the ideal generated by `const` $\pi_l$ and $U$ for some unit $\gamma$, and symmetrically with $R_2, x_2, U^n$ and the ideal generated by `const` $\pi_l$ and $V$. Finally, the $R_1$-residue of $x$ vanishes while the $R_2$-residue of $x$ has $x_2$-order $1$, and the $R_2$-residue of $y$ vanishes while the $R_1$-residue of $y$ has $x_1$-order $1$. The conclusion is twofold: first, every $f \in \mathcal N$ lying in both $R_1.\mathrm{integers}$ and $R_2.\mathrm{integers}$ has $x_1$-order of its $R_1$-residue $\ge 0$ whenever that residue is nonzero, and likewise for $x_2$ and its $R_2$-residue; second, for every $f \in F$ and $e_1, e_2 \in L$ with $e_1 f \in R_1.\mathrm{integers}$, $e_2 f \in R_2.\mathrm{integers}$ and both residues nonzero, the set of $P \in S$ with $P.\mathrm{ord}\, f \neq 0$ is finite and $\sum_{P \in S} P.\mathrm{ord}\, f$ equals the sum of the $x_1$-order of the $R_1$-residue of $e_1 f$ and the $x_2$-order of the $R_2$-residue of $e_2 f$.
--
--   This is the node annulus engine in its layered form: from crossing presentations of the completed local rings over each layer of constants it extracts both the regularity of residues at the two branch points and the slope law expressing the total order of $f$ along the annulus $S$ as the sum of the orders of its two boundary residues. It is used in the construction of tube and annulus charts on modular curves at the full level, in the statements asserting the existence of charted tube annuli with inert nodes, node rings and node charts.
